From a90da0b863073142f50c364cb42b77c2a9061b9e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 08:56:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Port the EfficientNet backbone to eager TensorFlow 2 / Keras

backbones/efficientnet_model.py and backbones/efficientnet_builder.py are a
vendored copy of the tensorflow/tpu EfficientNet, 826 lines carrying 29
tf.compat.v1 references and, through nnets/utils.py, the only two imports of
tensorflow.python.tpu in DeepLabCut.  They use no tf_slim at all, so the whole
backbone fits in one eager tf.keras.Model.

backbones/efficientnet_keras.py is that model.  It contains no tf.compat.v1,
no private TPU module, no variable_scope, no placeholder and no Session.  It
registers as KerasBackboneFactory("efficientnet"), takes raw images and does
its own normalisation -- cfg["mean_pixel"] subtracted, then STDDEV_RGB divided
out, which is what PoseEfficientNet.extract_features does -- and returns
(net, end_points).  nnets/efficientnet.py gains PoseEfficientNetKeras, a
BaseKerasPoseNet registered for KERAS_ENGINE, so test(), inference(),
add_inference_layers() and train() are inherited unchanged.  The TF1 path is
untouched and stays the default; resolve_engine picks between them.

What the TPU import was for

  nnets/utils.TpuBatchNormalization is cross-replica batch norm: on a TPU pod
  each replica sees a shard of the batch, and it sums the per-shard mean and
  mean-of-square across replicas with tpu_ops.cross_replica_sum so that all
  replicas normalise by the whole batch's statistics.  Off a TPU there are no
  replicas, tpu_function.get_tpu_context().number_of_shards is 1, and the
  class reduces to its base class.  Nothing ever built it: efficientnet_builder
  passes BatchNormalization and names the TPU class only in a comment, and
  patch 0018 had to fix a TypeError in its _moments -- proof the code path has
  never run.  It is deleted here, and with it the two tensorflow.python.tpu
  imports, which every DeepLabCut TensorFlow import was executing.  Nothing is
  lost for CPU or GPU use; TPU training would have to reintroduce it, and would
  today write it with tf.distribute rather than the private module.

  The published cloud-tpu-checkpoints weights are unaffected: they are ordinary
  name-based checkpoints and the batch-norm statistics in them were computed by
  that path on Google's hardware, not by anything DeepLabCut runs.

  nnets/utils.BatchNormalization, DepthwiseConv2D and drop_connect move to
  backbones/efficientnet_model.py verbatim, their only consumer.  They cannot
  simply be swapped for their Keras equivalents where they stand: they are
  built inside tf.compat.v1.variable_scope, so they take their names from the
  scope, while a plain Keras layer takes it from the enclosing model -- every
  batch-norm and depthwise variable of the network would be renamed and no
  checkpoint would load.  The unit moves when this file does.  The move leaves
  the TF1 graph bit-for-bit identical: same 308 (b0) and 493 (b3) variables,
  same names, same creation order, 0 update ops with use_batch_norm=False and
  96 / 154 with it, before and after.

Two properties are reproduced rather than approximated.

Variable naming.  Every layer knows the TF1 variable scope it stands in for
and reports it from slim_weights(), including the per-scope layer numbering
tf.compat.v1 generates -- conv2d, conv2d_1, tpu_batch_normalization_2, and the
separate counter of the nested se scope.  Against the tf_slim graph, for all
seven net_types DeepLabCut offers:

  efficientnet-b0  308 variables   b1 436   b2 436   b3 493
  efficientnet-b4  607             b5 735   b6 849

every name and shape identical, in the same creation order, 0 unmapped
weights, and the endpoint dictionaries identical too (42, 56, 56, 62, 74, 88
and 100 entries).  Trainable counts match as well: 212, 300, 300, 339, 417,
505, 583.  Checkpoints therefore convert mechanically in both directions:
save_tf1_checkpoint output restores into the unmodified tf_slim graph with
tf.compat.v1.train.Saver (308/308 variables), a snapshot that graph writes
loads back into the Keras net (308/308, 0 missing, 0 unused, max |diff| 0.0),
and compare_to_tf1_checkpoint reports 0.0 on the round trip.

The published ImageNet weights need two renames that no other backbone does:
they carry neither the efficientnet/ scope nor the raw variables, only their
/ExponentialMovingAverage shadow copies, which is the rename core/train.py
performs for the TF1 graph.  nnets/checkpoint.load_tf1_checkpoint grows a
rename hook for it rather than this file growing a private loader, and
nnets/efficientnet.imagenet_ckpt_name is that hook.  All 304 backbone weights
of efficientnet-b0 load, 0 missing, 0 mismatched.

Batch norm.  PoseEfficientNet.get_net ignores is_training and defaults
use_batch_norm to False, so the backbone normalises with the stored moving
averages and never updates them, while training as much as at inference; the
TF1 graph has zero UPDATE_OPS to say the same thing.  That is reproduced, not
corrected -- it is why the port needs none of the per-image batch-norm
machinery the MobileNet one does, and why an image's prediction cannot depend
on the images sharing its batch.  A tf.function traced on [None, H, W, 3] gives
bit-identical output for an image at batch sizes 2, 4 and 8 whatever the
companions are; only the batch size itself moves the result, by 8e-7 relative,
which is the convolution kernels blocking differently and happens with oneDNN
off as well.

Equivalence against the tf_slim implementation, all seven net_types at batch
sizes 1 and 4, 46 to 104 tensors each (every endpoint, the feature map and the
part_pred and locref heads), 128x160 input, real ImageNet weights loaded on
both sides from the same checkpoint:

  worst relative deviation   4.97e-6, always on a block's expansion_output
  keypoints through predict.argmax_pose_predict
                             max 2.4e-5 px, mean 3.6e-6 to 7.3e-6 px
  peak likelihoods           max 4.5e-7
  argmax cell                identical for 140 of 140 keypoints

That residual is the float32 noise floor, not a defect of the port.  Running
the very same Keras code in float64 moves the feature map by 9.6e-7 and
part_pred by 1.4e-6, against 1.4e-6 and 1.7e-6 for the gap to tf_slim; and
re-associating one line of the port's own arithmetic -- the squeeze-and-excite
spatial mean summed in two passes instead of one reduce_mean, algebraically
identical -- moves them by 7.5e-7 and 1.3e-6.  Evaluating the port in float64
does not bring it closer to tf_slim's float32 result (1.41e-6 against 1.38e-6),
which places the remainder in tf_slim's own rounding.  Two correct float32
implementations of this network cannot agree more closely than this.

Landmines checked rather than assumed.  The backbone carries no weight
regularisation at all -- the original convolutions pass no kernel_regularizer,
because EfficientNet's weight decay lives in the optimizer -- so the Keras
model adds none, and the pose net has exactly the two regularization losses
the tf_slim graph has, both from the heads, both through
slim_l2_regularizer's factor of a half.  Batch norm is pinned to
efficientnet_builder's momentum of 0.99 and epsilon of 1e-3 rather than left
on the Keras defaults, which agree today.  conv_kernel_initializer is kept as
it is, including computing fan_out from a depthwise kernel's trailing 1.
tf.compat.v1.div becomes tf.math.divide in drop_connect, and
tf.compat.v1.logging.info becomes tf.get_logger().info in the fourteen places
the two TF1 files log from.

Multi-animal eager training on EfficientNet works with this: core/train_eager
builds the net through the factory, restores the ImageNet backbone through the
rename hook (304/304), agrees with the score-map size patch 0016 derives (26
for a 200 px input at stride 8, which is what the network emits), and three
optimisation steps move the loss and the weights.  It was failing at
PoseNetFactory.create for want of a registered Keras net.

Not done here.  backbones/efficientnet_model.py and efficientnet_builder.py
stay in the tree, with 15 and 2 tf.compat.v1 references, because the TF1 path
stays selectable and because PoseMultiNet's own efficientnet branch calls
eff.build_model_base; they and the one reference left in nnets/efficientnet.py
retire together with the TF1 engine.  This patch takes the tree from 114
tf.compat.v1 references to 99 and removes the last import of
tensorflow.python.tpu.
